"(PDF) Spectral Similarity Measure Using Frequency Spectrum for Hyperspectral Image Classification (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/264743770_Spectral_Similarity_Measure_Using_Frequency_Spectrum_for_Hyperspectral_Image_Classification)\nciteturn164search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.8 years ago; Crawled: last month; # Spectral Similarity Measure Using Frequency Spectrum for Hyperspectral Image Classification ... A novel spectral similarity measure approach, which is named spectral frequency spectrum difference (SFSD), is proposed for hyperspectral image classification based on the frequency spectrum of spectral signature using the Fourier transform.\nArticle PDF Available\n\n# Spectral Similarity Measure Using Frequency Spectrum for Hyperspectral Image Classification\n\n  * January 2015\n  * IEEE Geoscience and Remote Sensing Letters 12(1):130-134\n\nDOI:10.1109/LGRS.2014.2329183\n\nAuthors:\n\nKe Wang\n\n  * Hohai University\n\nBin Yong\n\nBin Yong\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nXingfa Gu\n\n  * Guangzhou University\n\nPengfeng Xiao\n\n  * Nanjing University\n\nShow all 5 authors Hide\n\nImage\n\nDownload full-text PDFRead full-text\n\n## Abstract and Figures\n\nA novel spectral similarity measure approach, which is named spectral frequency spectrum difference (SFSD), is proposed for hyperspectral image classification based on the frequency spectrum of spectral signature using the Fourier transform. Many important characteristics of spectral signature can be clearly reflected in the frequency spectrum. Therefore, the spectral similarity is defined as the frequency spectrum's difference between the target and reference signatures. The frequency spectrum analysis in this study suggests that the magnitude values of the first few low-frequency components for spectral signature can effectively represent the spectral similarity. To balance the difference between the low- and high-frequency components, the frequency spectrum of the target spectral signature is taken as the normalized factor in the SFSD method. Next, the U.S. Geological Survey spectral data and two hyperspectral remote sensing images were employed as test data in our validation experiments. The new SFSD proposed here was compared with the leading approaches in terms of the spectral discriminability and classification accuracy. Results show that the SFSD exhibits a relatively better performance and has more robust applications for hyperspectral image classification.\n\n(First column) Original spectral signatures of smectite and xylan. (Second column) Reconstruction results with the sum of the first two harmonics . (Third column) Reconstruction results with the sum of the first ten harmonics. (Fourth column) Reconstruction results with the sum of the first 30 harmonics.… \n\n(First column) Original spectral signatures of water, anorthite, roscoelite, jarosite, and lawn grass. (Second column) Corresponding frequency spectra. (Third column) Frequency spectrum energy ratios (FS energy ratios) of the first nth harmonics to all harmonics. (Fourth column) Reconstruction by the first 46th harmonics.… \n\nquency spectrum analysis. First, these signatures are resampled\n\nwith the interval of 0.002 μ m and length (see the ﬁrst column\n\nin Fig. 2) of 1385. The frequency spectra of these signatures\n\nare shown in the second row in Fig. 2. Note that most of\n\nthe frequency spectrum energy is concentrated around direct\n\ncurrent (dc) component and the energy of high frequency is\n\nmuch less than that of low frequency.\n\nAs shown in Fig. 2, there are remarkable differences of\n\nthe frequency spectrum characteristics from these ﬁve typical\n\nspectral signatures. For example, from the frequency spectrum\n\nof water’s spectral signature, its dc component is much lower\n\nthan those of the other ground objects. Moreover, the distri-\n\nbution of the frequency spectrum is different among these ﬁve\n\nspectral signatures. Thus, the difference of magnitude spectrum\n\n(DMS) at the i th frequency components between the target and\n\nreference spectral signatures is deﬁned as the spectral similarity\n\nmeasure, which is expressed by\n\nDMS =\n\nn\n\n\n\ni=1 |MoTS(i)−MoRS(i)|(4)\n\n132 IEEE GEOSCIENCE AND REMOTE SENSING LETTERS, VOL. 12, NO. 1, JANUARY 2015\n\nwhere i means the i th frequency component; n is the length\n\nof the spectral signature; and MoTS(i)and MoRS(i)are the\n\nmagnitude of the i th frequency component for the target and\n\nreference spectral signatures, respectively.\n\nThe frequency spectrum descends from the dc component\n\nto the higher frequency component; thus, the DMS becomes\n\nsmaller when parameter i increases. It may result in that\n\nthe difference between target and reference spectra at higher\n\nfrequency components is ignored. Thus, it is necessary to\n\nnormalize the DMS to obtain a better measure. The spectral\n\nfrequency spectrum difference (SFSD) can be deﬁned as\n\nSFSD =\n\nN\n\n\n\ni=1\n\n|MoTS(i)−MoRS(i)|\n\nMoTS(i)(5)\n\nwhere N is the number of frequency components involved in\n\nthe measure.\n\nBecause the frequency spectrum is symmetric with respect\n\nto the dc component (see the second column in Fig. 2), N\n\nin (5) should be shorter than half the length of the spectral\n\nsignature. Meanwhile, the components involved in the SFSD\n\nmethod should begin from the dc component to the high-order\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Exemplar Component Analysis: A Fast Band Selection Method for Hyperspectral Imagery (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/272390797_Exemplar_Component_Analysis_A_Fast_Band_Selection_Method_for_Hyperspectral_Imagery)\nciteturn164search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; # Exemplar Component Analysis: A Fast Band Selection Method for Hyperspectral Imagery\n\n# Exemplar Component Analysis: A Fast Band Selection Method for Hyperspectral Imagery\n\n  * May 2015\n  * IEEE Geoscience and Remote Sensing Letters 12(5):998-1002\n\nDOI:10.1109/LGRS.2014.2372071\n\nAuthors:\n\nKang Sun\n\n  * Chinese Academy of Sciences\n\nXiurui Geng\n\n  * Chinese Academy of Sciences\n\nLuyan Ji\n\n  * Tsinghua University\n\nDownload full-text PDF\n\n## Abstract\n\nHow to find the representative bands is a key issue in band selection for hyperspectral data. Very often, unsupervised band selection is associated with data clustering, and the cluster centers (or exemplars) are considered ideal representatives. However, partitioning the bands into clusters may be very time-consuming and affected by the distribution of the data points. In this letter, we propose a new band selection method, i.e., exemplar component analysis (ECA), aiming at selecting the exemplars of bands. Interestingly, ECA does not involve actual clustering. Instead, it prioritizes the bands according to their exemplar score, which is an easy-to-compute indicator defined in this letter measuring the possibility of bands to be exemplars. As a result, ECA is of high efficiency and immune to distribution structures of the data. The experiments on real hyperspectral data set demonstrate that ECA is an effective and efficient band selection method.\n\n## Citations (127)\n\n## References (18)\n\n... Ranking-based methods assess each band individually using specific metrics, then select bands according to their respective scores. Exemplar component analysis (ECA) [10], for instance, prioritizes bands with higher local density and relatively larger distance from other high-density bands. Ji et al. [11] utilized the normalized matched filter weight to evaluate each band's contribution to target detection, facilitating the elimination of bands with lower significance. ...--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSpectral Similarity Measure Using Frequency Spectrum for Hyperspectral Image Classification — Lacuna (https://lacuna.tiptreesyste